OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Modularity-of-elliptic-curves-over-imaginary-quadratic-fields-October-4-2026", "title": "Modularity of elliptic curves over imaginary quadratic fields", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Modularity-of-elliptic-curves-over-imaginary-quadratic-fields-October-4-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "030", "family_title": "Modularity of elliptic curves over imaginary quadratic fields", "abstract_html": "We prove the modularity conjecture for elliptic curves over imaginary quadratic fields: every such curve is modular, with matching local parameters at every place.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K\\) be an imaginary quadratic number field and let \\(E/K\\) be an elliptic curve.</p><p class=\"li\">For a number field \\(L\\), write \\(G_L=\\operatorname{Gal}(\\overline L/L)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the cohomological realization\n  \\[\n  H^1(X,\\mathbf Q_l)=H^1_{\\mathrm{\\acute et}}(X_{\\overline L},\\mathbf Q_l).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The \\(l\\)-adic cyclotomic character \\(\\epsilon_l\\) has Hodge–Tate weight \\(-1\\). Thus \\(H^1(E,\\mathbf Q_l)\\) has labeled Hodge–Tate weights \\(0,1\\) and determinant \\(\\epsilon_l^{-1}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Automorphic representations are in unitary normalization. Weight zero means the algebraic weight corresponding to trivial coefficients.</p><p class=\"li\">For a regular algebraic cuspidal automorphic representation \\(\\pi\\), use the associated semisimple representation \\(r_\\iota(\\pi)\\), normalized by\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{rec}_{L_v}^{T}(\\pi_v)\n  :=\n  \\operatorname{rec}_{L_v}\n  \\bigl(\\pi_v\\otimes|\\det|_v^{-1/2}\\bigr),\n  \\]\n  with geometric Frobenius in local reciprocity.</p><p class=\"li\">In the CM isobaric case, \\(r_\\iota\\) means the sum of the corresponding character realizations with this same Tate normalization.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an isobaric automorphic representation \\(\\pi_E\\) of\n\\(\\mathrm{GL}_2(\\mathbf A_K)\\), with trivial central character and weight zero, such that, for every prime \\(l\\) and every isomorphism\n\\(\\iota:\\overline{\\mathbf Q}_l\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\mathbf C\\),\n\\[\nr_\\iota(\\pi_E)\n\\simeq\nH^1_{\\mathrm{\\acute et}}(E_{\\overline K},\\overline{\\mathbf Q}_l).\n\\]\nAt every finite place \\(v\\nmid l\\),\n\\[\n\\iota\\,\\mathrm{WD}\n\\left(\nH^1_{\\mathrm{\\acute et}}(E_{\\overline K},\\overline{\\mathbf Q}_l)\n\\big|_{G_{K_v}}\n\\right)^{\\mathrm{F\\text{-}ss}}\n\\simeq\n\\operatorname{rec}_{K_v}\n\\bigl(\\pi_{E,v}\\otimes|\\det|_v^{-1/2}\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe unitary parameter at the complex place is\n\\[\nz\\longmapsto\n\\operatorname{diag}\\left(\\frac z{|z|},\\frac{\\overline z}{|z|}\\right).\n\\]\nIn particular, every local factor agrees, and\n\\[\nL(E/K,s)=L(\\pi_E,s-1/2).\n\\]\nIf \\(E\\) has no complex multiplication, \\(\\pi_E\\) is cuspidal. In the CM case, it is given by automorphic induction of a Hecke character, or by an isobaric sum when the CM field is already \\(K\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>First suppose \\(E/K\\) is non-CM. Choose \\(p&gt;100\\), put \\(n=3p\\) and \\(F=K(\\mu_n)\\), and select \\(t\\) as in Propositions 3, 10, and 11. Proposition 13 gives automorphy of \\(B_t/F\\).</p><p>Since \\(F/K\\) is abelian, choose a tower from \\(K\\) to \\(F\\) with cyclic prime-degree steps. Each intermediate field is abelian over \\(\\mathbf Q\\) and contains the imaginary quadratic field \\(K\\), hence is CM.</p><p>The representation \\(H^1(B_t,\\overline{\\mathbf Q}_p)\\) is absolutely irreducible over \\(F\\), since its residual representation is already absolutely irreducible there by Proposition 11. Apply Proposition 14 down the tower. This gives automorphy of \\(B_t/K\\).</p><p>The curve \\(B_t\\) is the quadratic twist of \\(E\\) by \\(h(t)\\). Twisting the automorphic representation by the same quadratic Hecke character gives a cuspidal weight-zero representation \\(\\pi_E\\) realizing \\(H^1(E)\\).</p><p>By Proposition 8, \\(\\pi_E\\) has trivial central character, realizes \\(H^1(E)\\) at every coefficient prime and every coefficient isomorphism, and has the required Frobenius-semisimplified Weil–Deligne parameter at each finite place, choosing a coefficient prime different from the residue characteristic. Thus all finite local factors match.</p><p>At infinity, a cuspidal representation is generic. The archimedean classification with weight-zero infinitesimal character gives the parameter\n\\[\nz\\longmapsto\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n\\overline z/|z|&amp;0\\\\\n0&amp;z/|z|\n\\end{pmatrix},\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\nequivalent to the ordering in the statement; the finite-dimensional alternative is not generic. Tate normalization gives the asserted local comparison and\n\\[\nL(E/K,s)=L(\\pi_E,s-1/2).\n\\]</p><p>Now suppose \\(E\\) has CM by an imaginary quadratic field \\(M\\). Its endomorphisms are defined over \\(KM\\). Indeed, their action on invariant differentials is faithful; the eigenvalues lie in \\(M\\) and are fixed by \\(G_{KM}\\). The main theorem of complex multiplication expresses the two one-dimensional constituents of\n\\(H^1(E)|_{G_{KM}}\\) as realizations of algebraic Hecke characters, with infinity types prescribed by the CM type [33].</p><p>If \\(M\\not\\subset K\\), Galois exchanges the two characters, and \\(H^1(E)\\) is induced from either one over the quadratic extension \\(KM/K\\). Automorphic induction gives the required representation of \\(\\mathrm{GL}_2(\\mathbf A_K)\\). If \\(M=K\\), take the isobaric sum of the two characters.</p><p>In either case, normalize the resulting representation by \\(|\\det|^{1/2}\\) so that its Tate-normalized parameter realizes \\(H^1(E)\\). Complex multiplication and local class field theory, together with local compatibility of quadratic automorphic induction in the first case, give the finite parameters and infinity types. Since\n\\(\\det H^1(E)=\\epsilon_l^{-1}\\), undoing Tate normalization gives determinant one for the local automorphic parameters, hence trivial central character.</p><p>Finally, in both cases the finite factors also agree when Weil–Deligne representations are formed at the residue-characteristic coefficient prime. For good reduction this is crystalline comparison.</p><p>For bad potentially good reduction, Weil–Deligne inertia is finite and nontrivial, with zero monodromy and determinant one. Its invariant subspace cannot have dimension one: a semisimple two-dimensional determinant-one representation with a fixed vector would be trivial. Thus it has no invariants and its local factor is one. At the residue-characteristic coefficient prime, nontriviality of inertia follows from the crystalline good-reduction criterion [12].</p><p>For potentially multiplicative reduction, the Tate-curve description, allowing a quadratic twist, gives the same special Weil–Deligne parameter at all coefficient primes. This completes the proof.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Lemmas 3.1, 3.2, and 3.5; Proposition 3.3; Remark 3.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be a number field, let \\(E/k\\) be an elliptic curve, and fix an odd integer \\(n\\geq3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  E:\\ y^2=h(x),\n  \\]\n  where \\(h\\in k[x]\\) is a separable cubic and the point at infinity is the origin \\(O\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(h(t)\\neq0\\), put\n  \\[\n  B_t:\\ h(t)y^2=h(x),\\qquad\n  R_t=(t,1),\\qquad S_t=[n]R_t.\n  \\tag{3.1}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U\\subset\\mathbf A_k^1\\) be the open set on which\n  \\(O,R_t,-R_t,S_t,-S_t\\) are pairwise distinct. Further finitely many points may be removed when necessary.</p><p class=\"li\">For a divisor \\(m&gt;1\\) of \\(n\\), let \\(C_m\\to U\\) be the cyclic-cover family constructed in Lemma 1.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Pull this family back along \\(t=x(R)\\) to the open subset of \\(E\\) where \\(O,\\pm R,\\pm[n]R\\) are distinct. The isomorphism\n  \\[\n  B_{x(R)}\\longrightarrow E,\\qquad\n  (x,y)\\longmapsto(x,y(R)y)\n  \\]\n  identifies the base elliptic curve with the constant curve \\(E\\). Write \\(C_{m,R}\\) and \\(f_R\\) for the resulting cover and normalized function.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The family has the dihedral action and quotient maps of Lemma 1.1 and the following two degenerations.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(k\\) contains \\(\\mu_m\\), at \\(R=O\\) the semistable special fiber is a rational curve joined at distinct points to \\(m\\) copies of \\(E\\), all defined over \\(k\\). Rotations cyclically permute the elliptic components. The Jacobian family extends to an abelian scheme on a Zariski neighborhood of \\(O\\) in \\(E_k\\), with fiber \\(E^m\\) at \\(O\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Over \\(\\mathbf C\\), choose an oriented period basis \\(a,b\\) of \\(E(\\mathbf C)\\) and put \\(R_*=a/(2n)\\). The semistable special fiber consists of a connected unramified degree-\\(m\\) elliptic cover \\(E'\\to E\\) and a rational component, joined at \\(m\\) distinct nodes. Every node has smoothing order one; more precisely, in analytic coordinates its equation is\n   \\[\n   XU=-2nt,\\qquad t=R-R_*.\n   \\]</p><p>The abelian scheme in assertion 1 is over a characteristic-zero parameter curve. No extension of the Kummer cover over a ring of integers, particularly in residue characteristic dividing \\(m\\), is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 1.1 constructs the family and its action, and Lemma 1.3 proves assertion 2.</p><p>For assertion 1, use the model of Lemma 1.2 at \\(R=O\\). With \\(v=z/r\\) on the exceptional line, its normalized function is\n\\[\nf_{\\mathrm{exc}}(v)\n=\n\\frac{v-n}{v+n}\n\\left(\\frac{v+1}{v-1}\\right)^n.\n\\tag{3.6}\n\\]\nIts divisor gives this expression up to a scalar. The section \\(Z=O\\) meets the exceptional line at \\(v=0\\), where the function has value \\(1\\). Since \\(n\\) is odd, the displayed expression also has value \\(1\\) there, so the scalar is \\(1\\).</p><p>The restriction of \\(f_R\\) to the main elliptic component has no divisor and is constant. Its value at the node is \\(f_{\\mathrm{exc}}(\\infty)=1\\). The cover of the main component is therefore the disjoint union of the \\(m\\) copies of \\(E\\) given by \\(Y\\in\\mu_m\\).</p><p>On the exceptional line, the substitution\n\\[\nY'=Y\\left(\\frac{v-1}{v+1}\\right)^{n/m}\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\nY'^m=\\frac{v-n}{v+n}.\n\\]\nIts \\(m\\) points above \\(v=\\infty\\) are defined over \\(k\\), and attach one each to the \\(m\\) elliptic components.</p><p>The dual graph is a tree. Thus the fiber is of compact type, and its identity-component Picard variety is \\(E^m\\). The marked point \\((O,1)\\) extends through the smooth locus. The relative Picard theorem for nodal curves of compact type makes the Jacobian family an abelian scheme across this fiber: the relative identity-component Picard scheme is semi-abelian, and its torus vanishes for a tree dual graph [7, Section 9.4, Theorem 1, and Section 9.2, Proposition 10]. Shrinking the parameter neighborhood removes the other singular fibers.</p><p>This construction is over an open subset of the characteristic-zero curve \\(E_k\\), as emphasized in original Remark 3.4. Arithmetic reduction types over completions are established separately below.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the setting of Proposition 1, with \\(m&gt;1\\) dividing \\(n\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The open set \\(U\\) is nonempty. There is a smooth proper family of geometrically connected genus-\\(m\\) curves \\(C_m\\to U\\), obtained by normalizing the elliptic family \\(B\\to U\\) in the extension written fiberwise as\n\\[\nY^m=f_t(Z),\\qquad\n\\operatorname{div}(f_t)\n=(S_t)-(-S_t)-n(R_t)+n(-R_t),\\qquad\nf_t(O)=1.\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nThe family has an involution\n\\[\nw:(Z,Y)\\longmapsto(-Z,Y^{-1}).\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\nOver a field containing \\(\\mu_m\\), the rotations\n\\[\nu_\\zeta:(Z,Y)\\longmapsto(Z,\\zeta Y)\n\\]\nare defined and satisfy \\(wu_\\zeta w=u_{\\zeta^{-1}}\\). If \\(d\\mid m\\), the map \\(Y\\mapsto Y^{m/d}\\) gives \\(C_{m,t}\\to C_{d,t}\\); for \\(d=1\\), the quotient is \\(B_t\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>After the double cover \\(t=x(R)\\), the displayed identification \\(B_{x(R)}\\to E\\) sends \\(R_t\\) to the moving point \\(R\\). Each excluded equality imposes a torsion condition on \\(R\\), hence excludes only finitely many points. Thus \\(U\\) is nonempty.</p><p>On an elliptic curve, a degree-zero divisor is principal precisely when its sum in the elliptic-curve group is \\(O\\). The divisor in (3.2) has sum\n\\[\nS_t-(-S_t)-nR_t+n(-R_t)=2S_t-2nR_t=O.\n\\]\nIt determines a rational function, uniquely after imposing \\(f_t(O)=1\\). Applying this over \\(k(U)\\) and spreading out gives a rational function on the family. Its divisor changes sign under \\(Z\\mapsto-Z\\), and normalization at \\(O\\) gives\n\\[\nf_t(-Z)=f_t(Z)^{-1}.\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]\nEvery fiber component of the smooth elliptic family is preserved by inversion, so its valuation is its own negative. The function therefore has no vertical divisor, and the prescribed disjoint sections are its entire divisor over \\(U\\).</p><p>The valuation at \\(S_t\\) is \\(1\\), so the Kummer extension has degree \\(m\\), even geometrically. It is totally ramified at \\(S_t\\) and \\(-S_t\\), and unramified elsewhere because the remaining nonzero valuations are \\(\\pm n\\), divisible by \\(m\\). Riemann–Hurwitz gives\n\\[\n2g(C_{m,t})-2=2(m-1).\n\\]</p><p>The same local description proves smoothness over \\(U\\). A zero or pole of order divisible by \\(m\\) can be removed from the Kummer equation. At a section of order \\(1\\) or \\(-1\\), after taking a root of a unit étale locally, the equation is the \\(m\\)-th-root cover of a parameter transverse to that section. Normalization is finite, so the resulting smooth family is proper. Equation (3.4) gives \\(w\\), and the remaining assertions follow from the Kummer equations.</p><p>Under pullback to the constant-\\(E\\) family, the defining functions agree exactly: their divisors agree and both have value \\(1\\) at \\(O\\). No scalar twist is introduced.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the constant-\\(E\\) family of Proposition 1. Consider either boundary parameter\n\\[\nR=O,\n\\]\nor\n\\[\nR=R_*,\\qquad\n[n]R_*\\in E[2]\\setminus\\{O\\},\\qquad\nO,\\pm R_*,[n]R_*\\ \\text{distinct}.\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]\nThe second boundary parameter may be considered after extending the ground field.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Blow up the surface at the collision point over the chosen boundary parameter and normalize in \\(Y^m=f_R\\). This gives a regular semistable model near that parameter. Its special fiber has \\(m\\) geometric nodes above the node of the blown-up elliptic surface, each with smoothing parameter of order one in a parameter on the base. The component above the exceptional projective line is a geometrically connected rational curve.</p>", "proof": "<p>At \\(R=O\\), choose parameters \\(r\\) on the parameter curve and \\(z\\) on the constant elliptic curve, both at \\(O\\), with the same tangent coordinate. The sections \\(R,-R,[n]R,-[n]R\\) have first-order equations\n\\[\nz=r,-r,nr,-nr.\n\\]\nTheir strict transforms meet the exceptional line at four distinct points with coordinates \\(1,-1,n,-n\\); the node joining that line to the main component has coordinate \\(\\infty\\).</p><p>At the second boundary parameter, only \\([n]R\\) and \\(-[n]R\\) collide. Their tangent slopes are distinct, so one blowup separates them from each other and from the node.</p><p>The involution \\(Z\\mapsto-Z\\) preserves both components of each blown-up fiber. Equation (3.4) forces the valuation of \\(f_R\\) on each component to be zero. No horizontal divisor section passes through the node. Since the surface is regular, \\(f_R\\) is a unit there, and taking its \\(m\\)-th root is étale. The downstairs node has local smoothing equation\n\\[\nxy=b,\n\\]\nwhere \\(b\\) is a parameter on the base. Over an algebraic closure, its \\(m\\) lifts have the same equation. Thus the smoothing parameter is \\(b\\), not a higher power.</p><p>At the separated sections, normalization has the smooth local description of Lemma 1.1. This proves regularity and semistability. On the exceptional line, only the two sections of multiplicities \\(1\\) and \\(-1\\) cause ramification. The cyclic cover is connected and totally ramified at those two points, and Riemann–Hurwitz gives genus zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the complex constant-\\(E\\) family of Proposition 1. Choose an oriented period basis \\(a,b\\) and put \\(R_*=a/(2n)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The special fiber of Lemma 1.2 consists of a connected unramified degree-\\(m\\) cover \\(E'\\to E\\) and a rational component, joined at \\(m\\) distinct nodes. In analytic coordinates near each node, the equation is\n\\[\nXU=-2nt,\\qquad t=R-R_*.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(Q=a/2=[n]R_*\\) and use additive analytic coordinates \\(t=R-R_*\\) and \\(z=Z-Q\\). The colliding sections are \\(z=nt\\) and \\(z=-nt\\); the other divisor sections avoid this neighborhood. Hence\n\\[\nf_R=g(z,t)\\frac{z-nt}{z+nt}\n\\]\nwith \\(g\\) a holomorphic unit. Choose a local \\(m\\)-th root of \\(g\\), and put \\(V=Y/g^{1/m}\\). Then\n\\[\n(z+nt)(V^m-1)=-2nt.\n\\tag{3.7}\n\\]\nFor every \\(\\zeta\\in\\mu_m\\), the function \\(U=V^m-1\\) is a local coordinate at \\(V=\\zeta\\). Taking \\(X=z+nt\\) gives the stated equation. This is the local chart of the normalized blowup: the exceptional component has \\(X=0\\), and the main component has \\(U=0\\). The nonzero constant \\(2n\\) changes no vanishing order.</p><p>On the main component, the limiting function has divisor\n\\[\nn(-R_*)-n(R_*).\n\\]\nThe associated unramified Kummer cover is determined by the \\(m\\)-torsion divisor class\n\\[\n\\frac nm\\bigl((-R_*)-(R_*)\\bigr)\n\\longleftrightarrow\n-\\frac{2n}{m}R_*=-\\frac am\n\\quad\\text{in }E(\\mathbf C).\n\\]\nThis class has exact order \\(m\\), so the cover is connected. An unramified degree-\\(m\\) cover of an elliptic curve is itself an elliptic curve \\(E'\\). The exceptional cover is rational by Lemma 1.2, and its \\(m\\) attachments are the nodes just computed.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Lemmas 4.1 and 4.4; Proposition 4.2; Theorem 4.5; Remark 4.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a complex elliptic curve \\(E\\), an odd integer \\(n\\geq3\\), and a divisor \\(m&gt;1\\) of \\(n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the constant-\\(E\\) family \\(C_{m,R}\\) of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\Gamma_m=\\mu_m,\\qquad\n  u:(Z,Y)\\longmapsto(Z,e^{2\\pi i/m}Y).\n  \\]\n  Here \\(\\mathbf Z[\\Gamma_m]\\) is the group ring, not the cyclotomic integer ring.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose an oriented period basis \\(a,b\\) and identify the oriented torus with\n  \\((\\mathbf R/\\mathbf Z)^2\\), with \\(a\\) horizontal and \\(b\\) vertical.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an integral symplectic basis \\(a_i,b_i\\), indexed by \\(i\\in\\mathbf Z/m\\mathbf Z\\), with \\(u(a_i)=a_{i+1}\\) and \\(u(b_i)=b_{i+1}\\). In particular,\n\\[\nH_1(C_m,\\mathbf Z)\\simeq\\mathbf Z[\\Gamma_m]^2,\n\\qquad\nH^1(C_m,\\mathbf Z)\\simeq\\mathbf Z[\\Gamma_m]^2.\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nIn this same basis, geometric monodromy contains \\(T_a,T_b\\) satisfying\n\\[\nT_a(a_j)=a_j,\\qquad\nT_a(b_j)=b_j+\\sigma_a(2a_j-a_{j-1}-a_{j+1}),\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\n\\[\nT_b(b_j)=b_j,\\qquad\nT_b(a_j)=a_j+\\sigma_b(2b_j-b_{j-1}-b_{j+1}),\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\sigma_a,\\sigma_b\\in\\{1,-1\\}\\) are independent of \\(j\\). Their off-diagonal group-ring entries are\n\\(\\sigma_a(2-u-u^{-1})\\) and \\(\\sigma_b(2-u-u^{-1})\\).</p><p>These actions also occur in the geometric monodromy of the family over \\(U\\), under the pullback identification. After comparison,\n\\(H^1_{\\mathrm{\\acute et}}(C_m,\\mathbf Z_\\ell)\\) is free of rank two over\n\\(\\mathbf Z_\\ell[\\Gamma_m]\\) for every prime \\(\\ell\\), including \\(\\ell\\mid m\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.2 proves the integral assertions.</p><p>The Picard–Lefschetz formula for a positively oriented loop about a semistable fiber is the product of the Dehn twists at its nodes [18, Exposé XV]. By Proposition 1, each node has smoothing order one, so each twist has exponent one. The vanishing cycles are disjoint and have pairwise zero intersection. The product acts by\n\\[\n\\gamma\\longmapsto\n\\gamma+\\sigma\\sum_i\\langle\\gamma,\\delta_i\\rangle[\\delta_i],\n\\tag{4.7}\n\\]\nwith one sign \\(\\sigma\\) depending only on the intersection convention.</p><p>Substituting Lemma 2.3 gives zero on the \\(a_j\\), and, up to this one sign,\n\\[\n(a_j-a_{j+1})-(a_{j-1}-a_j)\n=\n2a_j-a_{j-1}-a_{j+1}\n\\]\non \\(b_j\\). This proves (4.5). Replacing the horizontal path by\n\\(R_s=(s/n)b\\) gives (4.6), initially in the basis from the vertical slit.</p><p>To compare the bases, choose both starting parameters sufficiently close to \\(O\\). The horizontal loop at \\(y=1/2\\) and the vertical loop at \\(x=1/2\\) avoid one fixed small disk about \\(O\\) and avoid both short slits. Outside that disk, the covering extends across \\(R=O\\) as \\(m\\) disjoint sheets. Trivialize it over a small simply connected parameter disk containing \\(O\\), and use this trivialization to label the lifts of the two fixed loops. Parallel transport identifies both sets of \\(a_i,b_i\\) with the same basis. Circling \\(O\\) introduces no ambiguity on these classes, since the sheet trivialization extends over \\(O\\). A different initial labeling makes only a common cyclic shift, leaving (4.5)–(4.6) unchanged.</p><p>Thus both operators belong to one geometric monodromy group. Pullback along the open parameter curve \\(E\\to U\\) maps its fundamental group into that of \\(U\\), so the operators occur in the latter image. Removing further finitely many points induces a surjection on fundamental groups and does not remove them.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Proposition 2 and the path\n\\[\nR_s=\\frac sn a,\\qquad 0&lt;s&lt;\\frac12.\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nThe branch points are \\(-sa,sa\\). Let \\(I_s\\) be the horizontal arc from \\(-sa\\) to \\(sa\\) through \\(O\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The cover \\(C_{m,R_s}\\to E\\) is trivial over \\(E\\setminus I_s\\). It is obtained by cutting \\(m\\) copies of \\(E\\) along \\(I_s\\) and joining adjacent banks cyclically. Crossing the slit changes the sheet index by \\(1\\) or \\(-1\\), according to orientation and the initial indexing.</p>", "proof": "<p>For small \\(s\\), enclose the colliding sections in a small disk about \\(O\\). By Proposition 1, outside that disk the model at \\(R=O\\) consists of \\(m\\) disjoint copies of its complement. If \\(D\\) is such a disk, the inclusion\n\\[\nE\\setminus\\operatorname{int}(D)\\hookrightarrow E\\setminus I_s\n\\]\nis a homotopy equivalence. The unramified cover of \\(E\\setminus I_s\\) is therefore trivial.</p><p>As \\(s\\) varies in \\((0,1/2)\\), the arcs \\(I_s\\) move by isotopy, and the topological isomorphism class of this unramified cover remains constant. Monodromy about either branch point is a generator of \\(\\Gamma_m\\), since its valuation in \\(f_R\\) is \\(1\\) or \\(-1\\). This gives the stated gluing.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Proposition 4.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Lemma 2.1. On each sheet let \\(a_i\\) be the horizontal loop at vertical coordinate \\(1/2\\), and let \\(b_i\\) be the vertical loop at horizontal coordinate \\(1/2\\). Orient them so that\n\\[\n\\langle a_i,b_j\\rangle=\\delta_{ij},\\qquad\n\\langle a_i,a_j\\rangle=\\langle b_i,b_j\\rangle=0.\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\nIndex the sheets so that \\(u\\) sends \\(a_i,b_i\\) to \\(a_{i+1},b_{i+1}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The \\(a_i,b_i\\) form an integral symplectic basis. For every smooth fiber,\n\\[\nH_1(C_m,\\mathbf Z)\\simeq\\mathbf Z[\\Gamma_m]^2,\n\\qquad\nH^1(C_m,\\mathbf Z)\\simeq\\mathbf Z[\\Gamma_m]^2.\n\\]\nThe corresponding étale cohomology is free of rank two over\n\\(\\mathbf Z_\\ell[\\Gamma_m]\\) for every prime \\(\\ell\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The genus is \\(m\\), so \\(H_1\\) has rank \\(2m\\). Equation (4.2) shows that the displayed \\(2m\\) classes are linearly independent and span a sublattice with unimodular intersection pairing. If its index were \\(d\\), its intersection determinant would be \\(d^2\\) times that of \\(H_1\\). Both determinants are \\(1\\), so \\(d=1\\). The deck action gives the homological group-ring isomorphism.</p><p>The dual of the integral regular module is again regular. Explicitly, the coefficient-of-identity pairing\n\\[\n\\mathbf Z[\\Gamma_m]\\times\\mathbf Z[\\Gamma_m]\\longrightarrow\\mathbf Z,\n\\qquad\n(x,y)\\longmapsto[1](xy)\n\\]\nis perfect. With the pullback action on the dual, it identifies\n\\(\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbf Z}(\\mathbf Z[\\Gamma_m],\\mathbf Z)\\) with\n\\(\\mathbf Z[\\Gamma_m]\\). This proves the cohomological assertion without dividing by \\(m\\).</p><p>Topological local triviality transports the result through the connected smooth parameter open, and comparison gives the étale assertion.</p><p>As in original Remark 4.3, the compact-type degeneration gives another explanation: its Jacobian has special fiber \\(E^m\\), with \\(\\Gamma_m\\) regularly permuting the factors. Its integral homology is therefore the regular module twice. The slit construction identifies cycles that can also be followed toward the second degeneration.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Lemmas 2.1 and 2.2. Let \\(s\\to1/2\\). The branch points meet at \\(a/2\\), while the slit fills all but a shrinking gap in the horizontal circle. Choose a small disk about \\(a/2\\) containing both branch points, with radius larger than their distance from the collision point. Its boundary has \\(m\\) closed lifts \\(\\delta_i\\), the neck circles at the nodes of Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After cyclic indexing,\n\\[\n[\\delta_i]=\\pm(a_i-a_{i+1}),\n\\qquad i\\in\\mathbf Z/m\\mathbf Z,\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\nor the same formula with \\(i-1\\) in place of \\(i+1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Arrange the boundary of the disk to cross the slit transversely twice. A lifted boundary circle has its upper arc on one sheet and its lower arc on an adjacent sheet. If the upper sheet is \\(i\\), the lower sheet is \\(i+1\\) or \\(i-1\\), by Lemma 2.1.</p><p>The circle is disjoint from every \\(a_j\\). It crosses the two corresponding vertical loops once each, with opposite intersection signs, and no other \\(b_j\\). These intersections determine its class in the integral symplectic basis of Lemma 2.2, giving (4.4).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 5.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K\\) be imaginary quadratic and let \\(E/K\\) be non-CM.</p><p class=\"li\">By Serre’s open image theorem [31, §4.4, Théorème 3], choose a prime \\(p&gt;100\\) such that the image of \\(G_K\\) on \\(E[p]\\) contains \\(\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbf F_p)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  n=3p,\\qquad F=K(\\mu_n),\\qquad M=\\mathbf Q(\\mu_n).\n  \\tag{5.1}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the families of Proposition 1, and fix \\(t\\in U(K)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\overline{\\mathbf Q}\\hookrightarrow\\mathbf C\\). For every prime \\(\\ell\\), choose\n  \\[\n  j_\\ell:\\overline{\\mathbf Q}\\hookrightarrow\\overline{\\mathbf Q}_\\ell,\n  \\qquad\n  \\iota_\\ell:\\overline{\\mathbf Q}_\\ell\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\mathbf C,\n  \\]\n  whose composition is the fixed complex embedding.</p><p class=\"li\">A rotation character is a homomorphism\n  \\(\\theta:\\mu_n(F)\\to M^\\times\\). Define\n  \\[\n  V_{\\theta,\\ell}\n  =\n  \\left\\{\n  x\\in H^1_{\\mathrm{\\acute et}}(C_n(t)_{\\overline F},\n       \\overline{\\mathbf Q}_\\ell):\n  u^*x=j_\\ell(\\theta(u))x\n  \\text{ for all }u\\in\\mu_n(F)\n  \\right\\}.\n  \\tag{5.3}\n  \\]\n  All deck transformations are defined over \\(F\\), so these are \\(G_F\\)-representations. Write \\(V_\\theta\\) for the collection of realizations.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The field \\(F\\) is an abelian CM extension of \\(\\mathbf Q\\), and \\(\\zeta_5\\notin F\\). For every \\(t\\in U(K)\\) and every \\(\\theta\\):</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(V_{\\theta,\\ell}\\) is semisimple of dimension two and determinant \\(\\epsilon_\\ell^{-1}\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. At every place above \\(\\ell\\), it is potentially semistable with labeled Hodge–Tate weights \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. There is a finite set \\(S\\) of places of \\(F\\), independent of \\(\\ell\\), such that \\(V_{\\theta,\\ell}\\) is unramified at \\(v\\notin S\\) of residue characteristic different from \\(\\ell\\). Its Frobenius characteristic polynomial is the image under \\(j_\\ell\\) of a polynomial in \\(M[T]\\), independent of \\(\\ell\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Both \\(K/\\mathbf Q\\) and \\(\\mathbf Q(\\mu_n)/\\mathbf Q\\) are abelian, so \\(F/\\mathbf Q\\) is abelian. Complex conjugation is central, its fixed field is totally real, and \\(F\\) has no real embedding because it contains \\(K\\). Thus \\(F\\) is CM.</p><p>The field \\(\\mathbf Q(\\mu_n)\\) is unramified at \\(5\\), so the ramification index of \\(F\\) at \\(5\\) is at most \\([K:\\mathbf Q]=2\\). A field containing \\(\\mathbf Q(\\zeta_5)\\) has ramification index divisible by \\(4\\) at \\(5\\). Hence \\(\\zeta_5\\notin F\\).</p><p>Proposition 2 and comparison give \\(\\dim V_{\\theta,\\ell}=2\\). Cup product\n\\[\n\\langle\\, ,\\,\\rangle:\nH^1(C_n)\\times H^1(C_n)\\longrightarrow\n\\overline{\\mathbf Q}_\\ell(-1)\n\\]\npairs the \\(\\theta\\)-space perfectly with the \\(\\theta^{-1}\\)-space. The involution \\(w\\) is defined over \\(F\\), preserves cup product, and exchanges these spaces. Therefore\n\\[\n[x,y]_\\theta=\\langle x,w^*y\\rangle\n\\]\nis a perfect \\(G_F\\)-equivariant pairing on \\(V_{\\theta,\\ell}\\), and\n\\[\n\\langle y,w^*x\\rangle\n=\n\\langle w^*y,x\\rangle\n=\n-\\langle x,w^*y\\rangle.\n\\]\nIt is alternating. A perfect alternating pairing on a two-dimensional space identifies its determinant with its target, proving\n\\(\\det V_{\\theta,\\ell}=\\epsilon_\\ell^{-1}\\).</p><p>For any embedding \\(F\\hookrightarrow\\mathbf C\\), let \\(a_\\theta\\) be the multiplicity of \\(\\theta\\) in holomorphic differentials. The holomorphic involution gives \\(a_\\theta=a_{\\theta^{-1}}\\). Hodge decomposition and complex conjugation identify the dimension of the full \\(\\theta\\)-space with\n\\(a_\\theta+a_{\\theta^{-1}}=2\\), so \\(a_\\theta=1\\). This works at every embedding and for every character. Comparison gives the labeled weights \\(0,1\\). Semistable reduction and semistable comparison [39] give potential semistability, which passes to the summands.</p><p>Outside a finite set, \\(C_n\\) has good reduction and the rotations extend to a smooth proper model. Smooth proper base change gives unramifiedness. In characteristic zero the projector is\n\\[\ne_\\theta\n=\n\\frac1n\\sum_{u\\in\\mu_n(F)}\nj_\\ell(\\theta(u))^{-1}u^*.\n\\]\nThe Lefschetz trace formula for a rotation composed with a positive power of Frobenius makes its trace on \\(H^1(C_n)\\) independent of \\(\\ell\\). Applying \\(e_\\theta\\) gives traces in \\(M\\), compatible under the \\(j_\\ell\\). Together with the determinant this gives the common characteristic polynomial.</p><p>Finally, \\(H^1(C_n)\\) is the first cohomology of its Jacobian. Faltings’ semisimplicity theorem [20, §5, Satz 3], extension of scalars, and passage to a \\(G_F\\)-stable direct summand give semisimplicity.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Proposition 5.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the fields, families, parameter \\(t\\), and systems of Proposition 3. Put\n\\[\nD_t=C_3(t)/\\langle w\\rangle,\\qquad A_t=\\operatorname{Jac}(D_t).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(\\theta\\) has order \\(m&gt;1\\) dividing \\(n\\), then \\(V_\\theta\\) is realized by the corresponding character space in \\(H^1(C_m(t))\\). For the trivial character,\n\\[\nV_{1,\\ell}\\simeq\nH^1_{\\mathrm{\\acute et}}(B_{t,\\overline F},\n\\overline{\\mathbf Q}_\\ell).\n\\]\nThe curve \\(D_t\\) has genus one, \\(A_t\\) is an elliptic curve over \\(F\\), and for either nontrivial order-three character \\(\\eta\\),\n\\[\nV_{\\eta,\\ell}\\simeq\nH^1_{\\mathrm{\\acute et}}(A_{t,\\overline F},\n\\overline{\\mathbf Q}_\\ell)\n\\]\nas \\(G_F\\)-representations, for every \\(\\ell\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The quotient \\(C_n\\to C_m\\) sends \\(Y\\) to \\(Y^{n/m}\\). Pullback on rational cohomology is injective, since its composition with trace is multiplication by the degree. Its image is the subspace fixed by the kernel of \\(\\mu_n\\to\\mu_m\\). The rank-two calculation of Proposition 2 identifies its order-\\(m\\) character summand with \\(V_{\\theta,\\ell}\\). The quotient \\(C_n\\to B_t\\) similarly identifies the trivial-character space.</p><p>On \\(H^1(C_3)\\), the trivial rotation space is \\(H^1(B_t)\\), where \\(w\\) acts as \\([-1]^*=-1\\). The involution exchanges the two nontrivial rotation spaces, each of dimension two. Their direct sum has a two-dimensional \\(w\\)-invariant subspace. Rational cohomology of a finite quotient is its invariant subspace, so\n\\[\nH^1(D_t)\\simeq H^1(C_3)^{w=1}\n\\]\nhas dimension two. Thus \\(D_t\\) has genus one, and \\(A_t\\) is an elliptic curve whether or not \\(D_t\\) has an \\(F\\)-rational point.</p><p>For \\(x\\) in the \\(\\eta\\)-space, projection of \\(x+w^*x\\) onto that space is \\(x\\). This gives a \\(G_F\\)-equivariant isomorphism from the invariant subspace to \\(V_{\\eta,\\ell}\\). Finally,\n\\(H^1(D_t)\\simeq H^1(\\operatorname{Jac}(D_t))\\). This identification is independent of the geometric base point in an Abel–Jacobi map because translations act trivially on cohomology.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Corollary 5.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a primitive character \\(\\chi\\) of order \\(n=3p\\), and write its unique primary decomposition as\n  \\[\n  \\chi=\\psi\\eta,\\qquad\n  \\operatorname{ord}(\\psi)=p,\\qquad\n  \\operatorname{ord}(\\eta)=3.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\overline V_{\\theta,\\ell}\\) denote the semisimplified reduction of a stable lattice, with scalars extended to \\(\\overline{\\mathbf F}_\\ell\\). This is independent of the lattice.</p><p class=\"li\">In a congruence at \\(\\ell\\), all characters use the same embedding \\(j_\\ell\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(t\\in U(K)\\),\n\\[\n\\overline V_{\\eta,p}\\simeq\\overline V_{\\chi,p},\n\\qquad\n\\overline V_{\\chi,3}\\simeq\\overline V_{\\psi,3},\n\\qquad\n\\overline V_{\\psi,p}\\simeq\\overline V_{1,p}.\n\\tag{5.4}\n\\]\nThese congruences hold with the elliptic-curve realizations of Proposition 4 at the two ends.</p>", "proof": "<p>A root of unity of \\(\\ell\\)-power order reduces to \\(1\\) in characteristic \\(\\ell\\). Thus \\(\\chi\\) and \\(\\eta\\) have the same reduction at \\(p\\), \\(\\chi\\) and \\(\\psi\\) have the same reduction at \\(3\\), and \\(\\psi\\) and \\(1\\) have the same reduction at \\(p\\). Lemma 5.1 gives all three congruences.</p><p>The rational identifications with smaller covers and elliptic curves in Proposition 4 preserve semisimplified reduction, even if their natural integral pullbacks have index divisible by \\(\\ell\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(t\\in U(K)\\) and a prime \\(\\ell\\), in the setting of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L/\\mathbf Q_\\ell\\) be finite and contain \\(j_\\ell(M)\\). Write \\(\\mathcal O\\) for its ring of integers, \\(\\mathfrak m\\) for its maximal ideal, and \\(k_\\ell\\) for its residue field.</p><p class=\"li\">For a rotation character \\(\\theta\\), put\n  \\[\n  T_\\theta=\n  H^1_{\\mathrm{\\acute et}}(C_n(t)_{\\overline F},\\mathbf Z_\\ell)\n  \\otimes_{\\mathbf Z_\\ell[\\mu_n],\\,j_\\ell\\theta}\\mathcal O.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The module \\(T_\\theta\\) is a free rank-two \\(G_F\\)-stable lattice realizing \\(V_{\\theta,\\ell}\\) after extension to \\(\\overline{\\mathbf Q}_\\ell\\). If \\(j_\\ell\\theta\\) and \\(j_\\ell\\theta'\\) have the same reduction as characters into \\(k_\\ell^\\times\\), then\n\\[\nT_\\theta/\\mathfrak mT_\\theta\n\\simeq\nT_{\\theta'}/\\mathfrak mT_{\\theta'}\n\\]\nas \\(k_\\ell[G_F]\\)-modules.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 2 and comparison give\n\\[\nH^1_{\\mathrm{\\acute et}}(C_n(t)_{\\overline F},\\mathbf Z_\\ell)\n\\simeq\\mathbf Z_\\ell[\\mu_n]^2\n\\]\nas group-ring modules. Galois commutes with the group ring because the rotations are defined over \\(F\\). Evaluation at \\(j_\\ell\\theta\\) therefore gives a free rank-two \\(\\mathcal O\\)-module with continuous Galois action.</p><p>In characteristic zero the group algebra is semisimple, and its quotient by character evaluation is the corresponding character summand. Hence the rational realization is \\(V_{\\theta,\\ell}\\). After reduction, the tensor product becomes\n\\[\nH^1_{\\mathrm{\\acute et}}(C_n(t)_{\\overline F},\\mathbf Z_\\ell)\n\\otimes_{\\mathbf Z_\\ell[\\mu_n],\\,\\overline\\theta}k_\\ell,\n\\]\nwhich depends only on the residual evaluation map. No assumption that \\(\\ell\\nmid n\\) is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Theorem 6.2, “Caraiani–Newton, specialized form.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The definition below is original Definition 6.1.", "statement": "<p>The definition below is original Definition 6.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A continuous representation\n  \\(\\overline r:G_L\\to\\mathrm{GL}_2(\\overline{\\mathbf F}_l)\\)\n  is decomposed generic if there is a rational prime \\(q\\neq l\\), splitting completely in \\(L\\), such that at every \\(v\\mid q\\) the representation is unramified and its Frobenius eigenvalues satisfy\n  \\[\n  \\alpha_v/\\beta_v\\neq q,\\qquad\n  \\beta_v/\\alpha_v\\neq q\n  \\quad\\text{in }\\overline{\\mathbf F}_l.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A two-dimensional \\(l\\)-adic representation is potentially ordinary of weight zero if, after a finite local extension, it has an invariant line with unramified character and quotient \\(\\epsilon_l^{-1}\\) times an unramified character.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L\\) be CM, let \\(l\\) be an odd prime other than \\(5\\), and let\n  \\[\n  r:G_L\\to\\mathrm{GL}_2(\\overline{\\mathbf Q}_l)\n  \\]\n  be continuous, unramified outside finitely many places, with determinant \\(\\epsilon_l^{-1}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At every \\(v\\mid l\\), suppose \\(r\\) is potentially semistable with labeled Hodge–Tate weights \\(0,1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(\\overline r\\) is decomposed generic and\n  \\(\\overline r|_{G_{L(\\zeta_l)}}\\) is absolutely irreducible.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose there are a cuspidal weight-zero regular algebraic representation\n  \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\mathrm{PGL}_2(\\mathbf A_L)\\) and an isomorphism\n  \\(\\iota:\\overline{\\mathbf Q}_l\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\mathbf C\\) with\n  \\[\n  \\overline{r_\\iota(\\pi)}\\simeq\\overline r.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">At each \\(v\\mid l\\), assume either:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. Both \\(r|_{G_{L_v}}\\) and \\(r_\\iota(\\pi)|_{G_{L_v}}\\) are potentially crystalline; one is potentially ordinary of weight zero if and only if the other is; and \\(\\operatorname{rec}_{L_v}(\\pi_v)\\) has zero monodromy; or</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. Neither local Galois representation is potentially crystalline, and \\(\\pi\\) is \\(\\iota\\)-ordinary of weight zero at \\(v\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Here automorphic \\(\\iota\\)-ordinarity is the ordinary Hecke condition used in [11, Theorem 5.2], imposed at sufficiently deep level and allowing ramified finite-order characters.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a cuspidal weight-zero regular algebraic representation\n\\(\\Pi\\) of \\(\\mathrm{PGL}_2(\\mathbf A_L)\\) such that\n\\[\nr\\simeq r_\\iota(\\Pi).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>This is the sufficient-hypothesis specialization of Caraiani–Newton [11, Theorem 5.2] stated in the paper. The numbering is that of the third arXiv version.</p><p>The inverse-cyclotomic determinant hypothesis permits \\(l=3\\), including residual image \\(\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbf F_3)\\); see [11, Remark 5.2.2]. No additional residual adequacy assumption is imposed. The applications below have \\(l=3\\) or \\(l=p&gt;100\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Theorem 6.3, “Elliptic starting point.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L\\) be a CM field not containing \\(\\zeta_5\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A/L\\) be a non-CM elliptic curve.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose its mod-\\(5\\) representation is decomposed generic, in the sense defined in Proposition 6, and absolutely irreducible on \\(G_{L(\\zeta_5)}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The system \\(H^1(A)\\), in the cohomological normalization of Theorem 1, is realized by a cuspidal weight-zero regular algebraic representation of\n\\(\\mathrm{PGL}_2(\\mathbf A_L)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>This is Caraiani–Newton [11, Theorem 6.1 and Lemma 6.1.3], after dualizing the Tate-module convention there when necessary. Absolute irreducibility and decomposed genericity in dimension two are preserved by duality.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Theorem 6.4, “Compatibility for modular elliptic curves.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D/L\\) be a non-CM elliptic curve over a CM field.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose a regular algebraic cuspidal representation \\(\\pi\\) realizes \\(H^1(D)\\) at one coefficient prime.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the cohomological and Tate-normalized conventions of Theorem 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The representation \\(\\pi\\) has weight zero and trivial central character. It realizes \\(H^1(D)\\) at every coefficient prime and every coefficient isomorphism, and\n\\[\n\\iota\\,\\mathrm{WD}\n\\left(\nH^1(D,\\overline{\\mathbf Q}_l)|_{G_{L_v}}\n\\right)^{\\mathrm{F\\text{-}ss}}\n\\simeq\n\\operatorname{rec}_{L_v}^{T}(\\pi_v),\n\\qquad v\\nmid l.\n\\]\nIf \\(D\\) has potentially multiplicative reduction at \\(v\\mid l\\), then \\(\\pi\\) is \\(\\iota\\)-ordinary of weight zero there.</p>", "proof": "<p>This is Caraiani–Newton [11, Lemma 6.1.3], in the paper’s normalization.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Proposition 7.3, “Local constancy of the semistable type.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L_0\\) be a finite extension of a \\(p\\)-adic field, let \\(X\\) be a smooth \\(L_0\\)-variety, and let \\(\\mathcal A\\to X\\) be an abelian scheme of relative dimension \\(g\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(x_0\\in X(L_0)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For an abelian variety \\(A\\), write\n  \\[\n  V_d(A)=T_d(A)\\otimes_{\\mathbf Z_d}\\mathbf Q_d.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are a finite extension \\(L/L_0\\) and an analytic neighborhood\n\\(\\mathcal N\\subset X(L_0)\\) of \\(x_0\\) such that every \\(\\mathcal A_x\\), for \\(x\\in\\mathcal N\\), is semistable over \\(L\\) and has the same toric rank as \\(\\mathcal A_{x_0}\\) over \\(L\\). If \\(\\mathcal A_{x_0}\\) has good reduction over \\(L\\), then \\(\\mathcal A_x\\) has the same Newton polygon over \\(L\\).</p><p>In particular, if \\(\\mathcal A_{x_0}\\simeq E_0^g\\) for an elliptic curve \\(E_0/L_0\\), all sufficiently nearby fibers have, after the same finite extension, the same one of:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. good ordinary reduction;</p><p class=\"li num\">2. good reduction with all Newton slopes \\(1/2\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">3. semistable reduction of toric rank \\(g\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose an odd prime \\(d\\) different from the residue characteristic. By semistable reduction, choose \\(L/L_0\\) such that \\(\\mathcal A_{x_0}\\) is semistable over \\(L\\) and all its \\(d\\)-torsion points are rational. Lemma 9.1, after base change to \\(L\\), makes all nearby fibers have rational \\(d\\)-torsion over \\(L\\).</p><p>These fibers are semistable over \\(L\\). Indeed, any inertia matrix on their \\(d\\)-adic Tate module has the form \\(1+dB\\), with \\(B\\) integral. Every eigenvalue \\(\\lambda\\) satisfies \\(v_d(\\lambda-1)\\geq1\\), where \\(v_d(d)=1\\). The local monodromy theorem makes \\(\\lambda\\) a root of unity. For odd \\(d\\), the only such root satisfying this inequality is \\(1\\): a root with nontrivial prime-to-\\(d\\) part has valuation zero, and a primitive \\(d^a\\)-th root has valuation\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{d^{a-1}(d-1)}&lt;1.\n\\]\nThus every inertia element is unipotent. Grothendieck’s criterion gives semistability [22, Exposé IX].</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal P\\) be the finite set of Lemma 9.2 for dimension \\(g\\) and the residue cardinality of \\(L\\). Choose \\(j\\) so large that reduction modulo \\(d^j\\) is injective on \\(\\mathcal P\\). Another application of Lemma 9.1 gives, on a smaller neighborhood,\n\\[\n\\mathcal A_x[d^j](\\overline L)\n\\simeq\n\\mathcal A_{x_0}[d^j](\\overline L)\n\\]\nas \\(G_L\\)-modules. Their full Frobenius polynomials are congruent modulo \\(d^j\\), hence equal. Lemma 9.2 gives equality of toric ranks and, in the good-reduction case, Newton polygons. The inverse image of this neighborhood in \\(X(L_0)\\) is a neighborhood of \\(x_0\\).</p><p>If \\(\\mathcal A_{x_0}\\simeq E_0^g\\), choose \\(L\\) so that \\(E_0\\) has good or split multiplicative reduction. An elliptic curve with good reduction is ordinary or supersingular, in the latter case with both slopes \\(1/2\\); in the multiplicative case \\(E_0^g\\) has toric rank \\(g\\). Enlarging \\(L\\) to rationalize \\(d\\)-torsion preserves these properties.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.1, “Local constancy of finite torsion.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L\\) be a local field of characteristic zero, let \\(X\\) be a smooth \\(L\\)-variety, and let \\(\\mathcal A\\to X\\) be an abelian scheme.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(x_0\\in X(L)\\) and an integer \\(N\\geq1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an analytic neighborhood \\(\\mathcal N\\) of \\(x_0\\) in \\(X(L)\\) such that\n\\[\n\\mathcal A_x[N](\\overline L)\n\\simeq\n\\mathcal A_{x_0}[N](\\overline L)\n\\qquad(x\\in\\mathcal N)\n\\]\nas \\(G_L\\)-modules.</p>", "proof": "<p>The group scheme \\(\\mathcal A[N]\\) is finite étale over \\(X\\), since the base has characteristic zero. Equivalently, the scheme of full level-\\(N\\) structures is a finite étale torsor under the constant group\n\\(\\mathrm{GL}_{2g}(\\mathbf Z/N\\mathbf Z)\\).</p><p>Choose a finite Galois extension \\(L'/L\\) over which the fiber of this torsor at \\(x_0\\) splits. The inverse function theorem for an étale morphism gives a local section through each point of the fiber over a neighborhood of \\(x_0\\) in \\(X(L')\\). After shrinking, these sections enumerate all points in every nearby fiber.</p><p>Intersecting the finitely many Galois conjugates of the neighborhood, and shrinking again if necessary, makes it Galois stable and preserves the Galois permutations of the sections. The right action of the finite level group likewise preserves the same permutations after shrinking. Restriction to \\(X(L)\\) identifies fibers as Galois sets with their level-group action, hence identifies the torsion modules.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "Lemma 7.2, “Finitely many semistable Frobenius polynomials.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a local field \\(L\\), with residue field of cardinality \\(Q\\), an integer \\(g\\geq1\\), and a prime \\(d\\) different from the residue characteristic.</p><p class=\"li\">For a semistable abelian variety \\(A/L\\) of dimension \\(g\\), let\n  \\[\n  P_A(T)=\\det(T-\\operatorname{Frob}_L\\mid V_d(A)),\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\operatorname{Frob}_L\\) is any lift of arithmetic Frobenius.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The polynomial \\(P_A\\) lies in \\(\\mathbf Z[T]\\), is independent of the lift and of \\(d\\), and ranges over a finite set depending only on \\(g,Q\\). It determines the toric rank of \\(A\\); if that rank is zero, it determines the Newton polygon of the good reduction.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(r\\) be the toric rank. Grothendieck’s semistable filtration identifies the graded pieces of \\(V_d(A)\\) with the rational Tate module of a rank-\\(r\\) torus, the rational Tate module of a good-reduction abelian variety of dimension \\(g-r\\), and a rank-\\(r\\) lattice [22, Exposé IX].</p><p>The torus character lattice and the lattice quotient have finite unramified Galois action. Arithmetic Frobenius eigenvalues on the toric and lattice pieces are respectively \\(Q\\) times roots of unity and roots of unity. The middle piece has \\(2(g-r)\\) finite-field abelian-variety eigenvalues, all of complex absolute value \\(Q^{1/2}\\).</p><p>Each graded piece has an integral characteristic polynomial independent of \\(d\\). For the toric and lattice pieces this follows from their finite-order integral actions and the toric Tate twist; for the abelian piece it is the usual finite-field Frobenius polynomial. Inertia acts trivially on the graded pieces, so their product is independent of the Frobenius lift.</p><p>Every root has complex absolute value at most \\(Q\\). The coefficient of \\(T^{2g-j}\\) is bounded in absolute value by\n\\[\n\\binom{2g}{j}Q^j.\n\\]\nThere are finitely many integral polynomials of fixed degree with these bounds.</p><p>Exactly \\(r\\) roots have absolute value \\(1\\), exactly \\(r\\) have absolute value \\(Q\\), and the remainder have absolute value \\(Q^{1/2}\\). These values are distinct because \\(Q&gt;1\\), so \\(P_A\\) determines \\(r\\). If \\(r=0\\), semistability is good reduction, and the normalized residue-characteristic valuations of the roots give the Newton slopes.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Lemma 7.4, Proposition 7.5, and Corollary 7.6", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K,E,p,n=3p,F=K(\\mu_n)\\), the covers, and the systems \\(V_{\\theta,\\ell}\\) be as in Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(J_t=\\operatorname{Jac}(C_n(t))\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Potential ordinarity of weight zero has the meaning in Proposition 6.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every place \\(v_0\\mid3p\\) of \\(K\\), there is a nonempty open subset\n\\(\\Omega_{v_0}\\subset U(K_{v_0})\\) such that, whenever \\(t\\in U(K)\\) lies in all these opens, the following hold at every place \\(v\\mid3p\\) of \\(F\\).</p><p>The variety \\(J_t\\) has the same one of these potential reduction types as \\(E^n\\):</p><p class=\"li num\">1. good ordinary reduction;</p><p class=\"li num\">2. good reduction with all Newton slopes \\(1/2\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">3. semistable reduction of toric rank \\(n\\).</p><p>The extensions realizing these types may be fixed in advance, independently of \\(t\\) in the indicated opens.</p><p>If \\(v\\) has residue characteristic \\(\\ell\\), then simultaneously for every rotation character \\(\\theta\\):</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(E\\) is potentially good ordinary at \\(v\\), then\n   \\(V_{\\theta,\\ell}|_{G_{F_v}}\\) is potentially crystalline and potentially ordinary of weight zero. Its distinguished weight-zero line is preserved by the original decomposition group.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(E\\) is potentially good supersingular at \\(v\\), then\n   \\(V_{\\theta,\\ell}|_{G_{F_v}}\\) is potentially crystalline and not potentially ordinary of weight zero.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If \\(E\\) is potentially multiplicative at \\(v\\), the monodromy of\n   \\(V_{\\theta,d}|_{G_{F_v}}\\) has rank one for every coefficient prime \\(d\\), including \\(d=\\ell\\). In particular \\(V_{\\theta,\\ell}|_{G_{F_v}}\\) is not potentially crystalline.</p>", "proof": "<p>First establish the open conditions, as in original Proposition 7.5.</p><p>By Proposition 1, the Jacobian of the constant-\\(E\\) cover extends near \\(O\\) in the parameter curve \\(E\\), with fiber \\(E^n\\). Fix \\(v_0\\mid3p\\). For every \\(v\\mid v_0\\), apply Proposition 9 over \\(F_v\\), obtaining a neighborhood of \\(O\\) in \\(E(F_v)\\) and a fixed finite extension of \\(F_v\\) on which the required reduction type holds. Their inverse images in \\(E(K_{v_0})\\) have an intersection which is a neighborhood of \\(O\\).</p><p>Choose \\(R_0\\neq O\\) in that intersection, sufficiently close to \\(O\\), away from the finitely many excluded parameters, and away from the ramification points of \\(x:E\\to\\mathbf P^1\\). The local inverse function theorem gives a nonempty neighborhood\n\\(\\Omega_{v_0}\\subset U(K_{v_0})\\) of \\(x(R_0)\\) and an analytic lift\n\\[\nR(t)=(t,y(t))\\in E(K_{v_0}),\\qquad y(t)^2=h(t),\n\\]\nremaining in the intersection. Shrink so that \\(y(t)\\neq0\\) and excluded parameters are avoided.</p><p>The isomorphism\n\\[\nB_t\\longrightarrow E,\\qquad\n(x,y)\\longmapsto(x,y(t)y)\n\\]\ncarries \\((t,1)\\) to \\(R(t)\\). The defining cover functions agree: their divisors agree and both have value \\(1\\) at \\(O\\). It therefore identifies the covers and Jacobians at every \\(v\\mid v_0\\). This proves the reduction assertions and the independence of the chosen extensions from \\(t\\).</p><p>Now establish original Corollary 7.6. Fix \\(v\\mid3p\\), of residue characteristic \\(\\ell\\), and choose a finite extension \\(L/F_v\\) giving the prescribed reduction.</p><p>In both good-reduction cases, crystalline comparison makes \\(H^1(J_{t,L},\\mathbf Q_\\ell)\\), and hence every character summand, crystalline.</p><p>In the ordinary case, the connected–étale sequence of the ordinary \\(\\ell\\)-divisible group, dualized to \\(H^1\\), gives a filtration with unramified subrepresentation and quotient \\(\\epsilon_\\ell^{-1}\\) times an unramified representation. It is functorial in endomorphisms and stable under rotations. On the rank-two summand \\(V_{\\theta,\\ell}\\), the unramified part contributes only weight \\(0\\), and the quotient contributes only weight \\(1\\). Proposition 3 therefore makes both parts one-dimensional.</p><p>After replacing \\(L\\) by a finite Galois extension, the weight-zero line is uniquely determined by its inertia action, since \\(\\epsilon_\\ell\\) has infinite image on the inertia of every finite extension. Conjugation by \\(G_{F_v}\\) preserves the line, so it descends to the original decomposition group.</p><p>If all Newton slopes are \\(1/2\\), the crystalline module of each summand has that same slope, unchanged by further finite extension. A potential ordinary filtration would give a crystalline rank-one subrepresentation of parallel Hodge–Tate weight zero. Weak admissibility would force its Newton slope to be zero, a contradiction.</p><p>Finally, in the totally toric case Lemma 10.1 gives monodromy rank \\(n\\) on the \\(2n\\)-dimensional \\(H^1(J_t)\\), for every coefficient prime. Monodromy commutes with rotations and preserves the \\(n\\) rank-two summands. Since \\(N^2=0\\) on first cohomology of a semistable abelian variety, each summand has rank at most one. Their ranks sum to \\(n\\), so all have rank one. Nonzero monodromy persists under finite extension and excludes potential crystallinity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>For this lemma, replace the global setting of Proposition 10 by:</p><p class=\"li\">a finite extension \\(L/\\mathbf Q_\\ell\\);</p><p class=\"li\">an abelian variety \\(A/L\\) of dimension \\(g\\) which acquires semistable reduction of toric rank \\(g\\) after a finite extension.</p><p>For \\(d=\\ell\\), use the Weil–Deligne representation attached to the potentially semistable representation by \\(\\ell\\)-adic Hodge theory.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every prime \\(d\\), the monodromy operator on the Weil–Deligne representation of\n\\(H^1_{\\mathrm{\\acute et}}(A_{\\overline L},\\mathbf Q_d)\\) has rank \\(g\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Monodromy rank does not change after finite extension. Assume \\(A\\) has split toric uniformization\n\\[\nA^{\\mathrm{an}}\\simeq(\\mathbf G_m^g)^{\\mathrm{an}}/\\Lambda,\n\\qquad \\Lambda\\simeq\\mathbf Z^g\n\\]\nwith \\(\\Lambda\\) rational over \\(L\\). Bases write the periods as\n\\((q_{ij})\\in(L^\\times)^{g\\times g}\\). Nondegeneracy of the valuation pairing says\n\\[\n\\bigl(v_L(q_{ij})\\bigr)_{1\\leq i,j\\leq g}\n\\text{ is nonsingular}.\n\\tag{7.1}\n\\]\nThis is the toric case of the semistable monodromy pairing [22, Exposé IX]; its de Rham realization is described in [12].</p><p>For \\(d\\neq\\ell\\), this pairing describes the monodromy map from the lattice quotient to the toric Tate-module subspace, giving rank \\(g\\).</p><p>For \\(d=\\ell\\), toric uniformization gives\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow\\mathbf Q_\\ell(1)^g\n\\longrightarrow V_\\ell(A)\n\\longrightarrow\\mathbf Q_\\ell^g\n\\longrightarrow0,\n\\tag{7.2}\n\\]\nwhose extension classes are the Kummer classes of the \\(q_{ij}\\). The representation is semistable by semistable comparison for abelian varieties [12].</p><p>Let \\(L_{\\mathrm{ur}}\\) be the maximal unramified subfield of \\(L\\). Monodromy is zero on the two outer terms, so it induces\n\\[\n\\nu:\nD_{\\mathrm{st}}(\\mathbf Q_\\ell^g)\n\\longrightarrow\nD_{\\mathrm{st}}(\\mathbf Q_\\ell(1)^g).\n\\]\nFrobenius on these spaces is respectively \\(\\sigma\\) and \\(\\ell^{-1}\\sigma\\), where \\(\\sigma\\) is Frobenius of \\(L_{\\mathrm{ur}}/\\mathbf Q_\\ell\\). The identity \\(N\\varphi=\\ell\\varphi N\\) makes\n\\(\\ker\\nu\\subset L_{\\mathrm{ur}}^g\\) stable under \\(\\sigma\\).</p><p>If \\(\\nu\\) had rank less than \\(g\\), finite Galois descent would give a nonzero\n\\(a=(a_j)\\in\\mathbf Q_\\ell^g\\cap\\ker\\nu\\). Pull (7.2) back along the line spanned by \\(a\\). The resulting extension of \\(\\mathbf Q_\\ell\\) by \\(\\mathbf Q_\\ell(1)^g\\) is semistable with zero monodromy, hence crystalline.</p><p>Under Kummer theory,\n\\(H_f^1(L,\\mathbf Q_\\ell(1))\\) is the kernel of the valuation map [6, Example 3.9]. Applied in each coordinate, this gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^g a_jv_L(q_{ij})=0\n\\qquad(1\\leq i\\leq g),\n\\]\ncontradicting (7.1). Therefore \\(N\\) has rank \\(g\\) on \\(V_\\ell(A)\\), and dualizing gives the same rank on \\(H^1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Proposition 8.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K,E,p,n=3p,F=K(\\mu_n),U\\), and the systems \\(V_{\\theta,\\ell}\\) be as in Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S_K\\) be a finite set of finite places of \\(K\\), containing all places above \\(3p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For each \\(v_0\\in S_K\\), prescribe a nonempty open subset\n  \\(\\Omega_{v_0}\\subset U(K_{v_0})\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The pairs requiring geometric monodromy are\n  \\[\n  (m,\\ell)=(3,p),(3,5),(p,3).\n  \\tag{8.1}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Decomposed genericity is as defined in Proposition 6, and residual systems are as in Proposition 5.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is one \\(t\\in U(K)\\), lying in every prescribed \\(\\Omega_{v_0}\\), such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. For every pair in (8.1) and every character \\(\\theta\\) of order \\(m\\),\n   \\(\\overline V_{\\theta,\\ell}|_{G_{F(\\zeta_\\ell)}}\\) is absolutely irreducible and \\(\\overline V_{\\theta,\\ell}\\) is decomposed generic over \\(F\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The same two properties hold for \\(\\overline V_{1,p}\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The elliptic curve\n   \\[\n   A_t=\\operatorname{Jac}(C_3(t)/\\langle w\\rangle)\n   \\]\n   is non-CM.</p><p>If the opens above \\(3p\\) are those of Proposition 10, the same parameter satisfies all its local conclusions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\nW=\\operatorname{Res}_{K/\\mathbf Q}U.\n\\]\nSince \\(U\\subset\\mathbf A_K^1\\) is open, \\(W\\subset\\mathbf A_{\\mathbf Q}^2\\) is open. Choose a finite Galois extension \\(D/\\mathbf Q\\) containing \\(F\\) and all \\(\\zeta_\\ell\\) for the residual characteristics in (8.1). If \\(c\\) is the nontrivial automorphism of \\(K/\\mathbf Q\\), then\n\\[\nW_D\\simeq U_D\\times(U^c)_D.\n\\]</p><p>For every pair in (8.1), pull back \\(H^1(C_m,\\mathbf F_\\ell)\\) from the first factor and \\(H^1(C_m^c,\\mathbf F_\\ell)\\) from the second. Their joint monodromy has finite image on \\(\\pi_1(W_D)\\). Take in \\(\\pi_1(W)\\) the normal core of the common kernel. The resulting finite quotient \\(H\\) records all these actions and has quotient \\(\\operatorname{Gal}(D/\\mathbf Q)\\).</p><p>Over \\(\\mathbf C\\), a loop in either parameter factor acts trivially on local systems from the other factor. Thus the transformations of Lemma 11.1 are independently available in both factors. For each \\((m,\\ell)\\), multiply the two factor loops supplied by that lemma to obtain a geometric element \\(h_{m,\\ell}\\) acting regular semisimply on every nontrivial character space in both factors. It maps trivially to \\(\\operatorname{Gal}(D/\\mathbf Q)\\). The quotient also records the individual opposite unipotents.</p><p>For each rational prime \\(r\\),\n\\[\nW(\\mathbf Q_r)=\\prod_{v_0\\mid r}U(K_{v_0}).\n\\]\nThe prescribed \\(\\Omega_{v_0}\\) give nonempty opens in these products; impose no extra condition on unprescribed factors. Lemma 11.3 gives \\(x\\in W(\\mathbf Q)\\), equivalently \\(t\\in U(K)\\), satisfying them and inducing a surjection \\(G_{\\mathbf Q}\\to H\\). Over \\(D\\), its coordinates are \\((t,t^c)\\). Smooth proper base change identifies the specialized systems with the residual cohomology of \\(C_m(t)\\) and \\(C_m^c(t^c)\\).</p><p>Equation (8.4) makes every chosen geometric element the image of an element of \\(G_D\\). In particular, the opposite unipotents survive over \\(G_D\\), proving absolute irreducibility there. As \\(G_D\\subset G_{F(\\zeta_\\ell)}\\), absolute irreducibility holds on the latter group. Since \\(\\ell\\nmid m\\), formation of rotation eigenspaces in integral cohomology of \\(C_m\\), after adjoining character values, commutes with reduction. These spaces are reductions of \\(V_{\\theta,\\ell}\\). Proposition 4 and independence of semisimplified reduction identify them with \\(\\overline V_{\\theta,\\ell}\\), including when defined using \\(C_n\\).</p><p>For decomposed genericity, fix \\((m,\\ell)\\) and choose \\(g\\in G_D\\) mapping to \\(h_{m,\\ell}\\). Every \\(\\sigma\\in G_{\\mathbf Q}\\) preserves or exchanges the two specialized covers according to its restriction to \\(K\\), and permutes rotation characters through its action on roots of unity. Thus \\(\\sigma g\\sigma^{-1}\\) on a character space of the first cover is conjugate to \\(g\\) on some character space of one of the two covers. All these spaces were required to be regular semisimple.</p><p>The specialization kernel defines a finite Galois extension of \\(\\mathbf Q\\) containing \\(D\\). Chebotarev for the conjugacy class of \\(h_{m,\\ell}\\) gives rational primes \\(q\\) splitting completely in \\(D\\). Discard \\(q=\\ell\\) and the finitely many ramified primes. At every \\(v\\mid q\\) of \\(F\\), Frobenius acts regular semisimply on each required space. The residue degree is one, and\n\\(q\\equiv1\\pmod\\ell\\) because \\(\\zeta_\\ell\\in D\\). Distinct eigenvalues give both ratio inequalities for decomposed genericity. A different \\(q\\) is allowed for each pair.</p><p>Lemma 11.2 proves assertion 2.</p><p>For assertion 3, use \\((m,\\ell)=(3,p)\\). The residual image of \\(V_{\\eta,p}\\), hence of \\(A_t\\) by Proposition 4, contains two opposite nonidentity unipotents. The representations are irreducible, so equality of semisimplifications identifies the representations themselves.</p><p>If \\(A_t\\) had CM, all its endomorphisms would be defined over an extension of \\(F\\) of degree at most two. Over that extension the characteristic-zero image lies in the commutative centralizer of the CM algebra, and its reduction is abelian. Thus the full residual image has an abelian normal subgroup of index at most two. Each unipotent has odd prime order \\(p\\), so both lie in this subgroup. But\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;a\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}\n\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\b&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}\n\\neq\n\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\b&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}\n\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;a\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}\n\\qquad(ab\\neq0).\n\\]\nThis is a contradiction.</p><p>Choosing the opens of Proposition 10 retains all its local conclusions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the families and pairs \\((m,\\ell)\\) of Proposition 11. Since \\(\\ell\\nmid m\\), residual cohomology decomposes into rotation-character spaces after extending its finite coefficient field.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On every nontrivial rotation-character space of \\(H^1(C_m,\\mathbf F_\\ell)\\), geometric monodromy is absolutely irreducible and contains opposite nonidentity root unipotents. One geometric monodromy element acts regular semisimply on all the nontrivial character spaces simultaneously. The same statements hold for the conjugate family over \\(K\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Work first with homology. On the character space with generator eigenvalue\n\\(\\lambda\\in\\overline{\\mathbf F}_\\ell^\\times\\), Proposition 2 gives\n\\[\nT_a=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n1&amp;\\sigma c_\\lambda\\\\0&amp;1\n\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\nT_b=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n1&amp;0\\\\\\tau c_\\lambda&amp;1\n\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\nc_\\lambda=2-\\lambda-\\lambda^{-1},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\sigma,\\tau\\in\\{1,-1\\}\\). Since\n\\[\nc_\\lambda=-\\frac{(\\lambda-1)^2}{\\lambda},\n\\]\nit is nonzero for \\(\\lambda\\neq1\\). The two matrices have different unique invariant lines, even over the algebraic closure, so their common representation is absolutely irreducible.</p><p>The product \\(T_aT_b^k\\) has determinant one and trace\n\\[\n2+\\sigma\\tau k c_\\lambda^2.\n\\tag{8.2}\n\\]\nIn odd characteristic, a determinant-one matrix is regular semisimple exactly when its trace is not \\(2\\) or \\(-2\\).</p><p>For \\(m=3\\), one has \\(\\lambda+\\lambda^{-1}=-1\\), so \\(c_\\lambda=3\\) for both nontrivial characters. Take \\(k=2\\). The possible traces are \\(20\\) and \\(-16\\), reducing modulo \\(5\\) to \\(0\\) and \\(4\\), neither of which is \\(2\\) or \\(-2\\). They also avoid \\(2,-2\\) modulo \\(p&gt;100\\).</p><p>For \\(m=p,\\ell=3\\), take \\(k=1\\). Failure of regular semisimplicity would force \\(c_\\lambda^2=4\\) or \\(-4\\), since \\(c_\\lambda\\neq0\\). Multiplication by \\(\\lambda^2\\) gives a degree-four polynomial over \\(\\mathbf F_3\\) satisfied by \\(\\lambda\\). Thus its degree over \\(\\mathbf F_3\\) is some \\(d\\leq4\\), and its order divides\n\\(3^d-1\\leq80\\), contradicting its prime order \\(p&gt;100\\). The same product therefore works for every nontrivial character.</p><p>The loops come from the constant-elliptic-curve parameter space. Proposition 2 places their images in geometric monodromy over \\(U\\). Duality preserves absolute irreducibility, nonidentity unipotents, and regular semisimplicity, giving the cohomological assertions. The argument applies to every complex elliptic curve, hence also to the conjugate of \\(E\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.2", "orig": "Lemma 8.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(K,E,p,F\\) and \\(B_t\\) from Proposition 3, with arbitrary \\(t\\in U(K)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The representation \\(\\overline V_{1,p}\\) is absolutely irreducible on\n\\(G_{F(\\zeta_p)}\\) and decomposed generic over \\(F\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\Gamma\\) be the image of \\(G_K\\) on \\(E[p]\\). Since \\(F/K\\) is abelian, the image of \\(G_F\\) contains \\([\\Gamma,\\Gamma]\\). The group \\(\\Gamma\\) contains \\(\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbf F_p)\\), which is perfect for \\(p&gt;3\\). Perfectness gives one inclusion in\n\\[\n[\\Gamma,\\Gamma]=\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbf F_p),\n\\]\nand the determinant map gives the other. Since \\(\\mu_p\\subset F\\), the determinant is trivial on \\(G_F\\), so\n\\[\n\\rho_{E,p}(G_F)=\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbf F_p).\n\\tag{8.3}\n\\]\nThe same holds for the conjugate curve \\(E^c\\), because \\(F/\\mathbf Q\\) is normal.</p><p>Let\n\\[\nJ\\subset\n\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbf F_p)\\times\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbf F_p)\n\\]\nbe the joint image on \\(E[p]\\) and \\(E^c[p]\\). Both projections are surjective.</p><p>There are exactly \\(p^2\\) matrices of trace \\(2\\) in \\(\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbf F_p)\\). Indeed, for\n\\(\\begin{pmatrix}a&amp;b\\\\c&amp;d\\end{pmatrix}\\),\nthe conditions give \\(d=2-a\\) and \\(bc=-(a-1)^2\\). For \\(a=1\\) there are \\(2p-1\\) choices for \\((b,c)\\), and each of the other \\(p-1\\) values gives \\(p-1\\) choices. Their sum is \\(p^2\\). Negation gives the same count for trace \\(-2\\).</p><p>Since \\(|\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbf F_p)|=p(p^2-1)\\) and each projection of \\(J\\) has equal-sized fibers, the proportion of elements whose image in at least one factor has trace \\(2\\) or \\(-2\\) is at most\n\\[\n2\\frac{2p^2}{p(p^2-1)}\n=\n\\frac{4p}{p^2-1}&lt;1.\n\\]\nThus some element of \\(G_F\\) acts regular semisimply on both representations.</p><p>Take a finite Galois extension of \\(\\mathbf Q\\) containing \\(F\\) and the fields needed for the two residual actions. Conjugation by \\(G_{\\mathbf Q}\\) preserves or exchanges the two curves and conjugates their matrices. The selected element and all its conjugates act regular semisimply on the representation of \\(E\\). Chebotarev gives rational primes \\(q\\), splitting completely in \\(F\\), with this property at every \\(v\\mid q\\). Exclude \\(p\\) and the finitely many bad-reduction primes. Since \\(\\mu_p\\subset F\\), \\(q\\equiv1\\pmod p\\); distinct eigenvalues give decomposed genericity.</p><p>The curve \\(B_t\\) is a quadratic twist of \\(E\\). Twisting multiplies residual matrices by scalar signs, preserving irreducibility and eigenvalue ratios. Excluding the finitely many primes ramified in the twist leaves the argument available. Finally \\(\\zeta_p\\in F\\), and dualizing \\(E[p]\\) gives the residual \\(H^1\\)-realization. Proposition 4 proves the claim.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.3", "orig": "Lemma 8.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>For this lemma, replace the family-specific setting by:</p><p class=\"li\">a nonempty open \\(W\\subset\\mathbf A_{\\mathbf Q}^{d}\\);</p><p class=\"li\">a finite Galois extension \\(D/\\mathbf Q\\);</p><p class=\"li\">a finite quotient \\(H\\) through which the constant-field map factors:\n  \\[\n  \\pi_1(W)\\twoheadrightarrow H\n  \\twoheadrightarrow\\operatorname{Gal}(D/\\mathbf Q).\n  \\]\nPrescribe nonempty open subsets of \\(W(\\mathbf Q_r)\\) for finitely many rational primes \\(r\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(x\\in W(\\mathbf Q)\\) in all prescribed opens such that its specialization\n\\(s_x:G_{\\mathbf Q}\\to H\\) is surjective. For this point,\n\\[\ns_x(G_D)\n=\n\\ker\\bigl(H\\to\\operatorname{Gal}(D/\\mathbf Q)\\bigr).\n\\tag{8.4}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The quotient \\(H\\) defines a connected finite étale Galois cover of \\(W\\). Hilbert irreducibility with weak approximation gives a rational point in the prescribed local opens whose fiber is connected [32, Proposition 3.3.1 and Theorem 3.5.3]. Connectedness of the Galois fiber is equivalent to surjectivity of \\(s_x\\). Geometric connectedness of the cover is not required.</p><p>The composition with \\(H\\to\\operatorname{Gal}(D/\\mathbf Q)\\) is the usual restriction map. Hence every element of its kernel has a preimage in \\(G_D\\), proving (8.4).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Lemmas 9.1 and 9.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(F\\), the systems \\(V_\\theta\\), compatible coefficient embeddings, and \\(J_t=\\operatorname{Jac}(C_n(t))\\) from Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose a weight-zero regular algebraic cuspidal representation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\mathrm{GL}_2(\\mathbf A_F)\\) realizes one member of \\(V_\\theta\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The representation \\(\\pi\\) realizes every member of \\(V_\\theta\\) using the compatible embeddings.</p><p>If, in addition, \\(\\pi\\) is a representation of \\(\\mathrm{PGL}_2(\\mathbf A_F)\\), \\(l\\in\\{3,p\\}\\), and \\(v\\mid l\\), then:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(J_t\\) has potentially good reduction at \\(v\\), \\(\\operatorname{rec}_{F_v}(\\pi_v)\\) has zero monodromy.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(J_t\\) acquires semistable reduction of toric rank \\(n\\) at \\(v\\), then \\(\\pi_v\\) is a quadratic or trivial twist of Steinberg, and \\(\\pi\\) is \\(\\iota_l\\)-ordinary of weight zero at \\(v\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 12.1 proves coefficient-prime transfer.</p><p>For the local assertions, assume the additional \\(\\mathrm{PGL}_2\\) hypothesis and choose a coefficient prime \\(a\\neq l\\). Lemma 12.1 identifies \\(r_{\\iota_a}(\\pi)\\) with \\(V_{\\theta,a}\\). Varma’s theorem [40, Theorem 1] identifies the underlying semisimplified Weil representations of its local Weil–Deligne parameter and \\(\\operatorname{rec}_{F_v}^{T}(\\pi_v)\\). Equality of monodromy is not assumed.</p><p>In the potentially good case, pass to an extension where \\(J_t\\) has good reduction. Every Frobenius eigenvalue on its \\(H^1\\), hence on the character summand, has complex absolute value the square root of the residue cardinality. Taking a positive Frobenius power shows that the two eigenvalues before extension also have equal absolute value. A rank-two Weil–Deligne representation with nonzero monodromy has eigenvalues differing by the norm character. They cannot have equal absolute value, so the automorphic parameter has zero monodromy. The Tate twist does not affect this conclusion.</p><p>In the totally toric case, Proposition 10 gives nonzero monodromy on \\(V_{\\theta,a}\\). Its semisimplified Weil representation is a sum of two characters differing by the norm. For this Weil representation, local Langlands for \\(\\mathrm{GL}_2\\) allows the special parameter with nonzero monodromy or the zero-monodromy parameter of a one-dimensional local representation. The latter is not generic. Every local component of a cuspidal \\(\\mathrm{GL}_2\\)-representation is generic, so \\(\\pi_v\\) is a Steinberg twist. Trivial central character forces its twisting character to have square one.</p><p>The calculation in the proof of [11, Lemma 6.1.3(4)], invoking the proof of [21, Lemma 5.6], makes a quadratic twist of Steinberg \\(\\iota_l\\)-ordinary of weight zero. It uses only this local representation and trivial central character. It applies also to ramified quadratic characters: the ordinary condition is imposed at sufficiently deep level, and a finite-order twist multiplies the relevant normalized Hecke eigenvalues by units.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "Lemma 9.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(V_\\theta\\) be one of the compatible systems of Proposition 3, with its fixed compatible embeddings. Let \\(\\pi\\) be weight-zero regular algebraic cuspidal on \\(\\mathrm{GL}_2(\\mathbf A_F)\\), realizing one member.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The representation \\(\\pi\\) realizes every member of \\(V_\\theta\\), using those compatible embeddings.</p>", "proof": "<p>Outside a finite set and away from the coefficient prime, Frobenius characteristic polynomials of \\(V_\\theta\\) are independent of that prime as algebraic polynomials with the fixed embeddings of character values. The good-place polynomials of \\(r_\\iota(\\pi)\\) have the same compatibility [25, 30]. Equality at the original prime therefore identifies the polynomials at every other prime.</p><p>Both representations are semisimple: for \\(V_\\theta\\), by Proposition 3 and Faltings’ theorem; automorphic Galois representations are taken semisimple. Chebotarev density and Brauer–Nesbitt give the isomorphism.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Proposition 9.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K,E,p,n,F\\), the characters \\(\\eta,\\psi,\\chi=\\eta\\psi\\), and the systems be as in Propositions 3 and 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(t\\in U(K)\\) by Proposition 11 using the local opens of Proposition 10.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(B=B_t\\) and \\(A=A_t\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The elliptic curve \\(B/F\\) is modular: \\(H^1(B)\\) is realized by a cuspidal weight-zero regular algebraic representation of\n\\(\\mathrm{PGL}_2(\\mathbf A_F)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>By Proposition 4, \\(V_\\eta=H^1(A)\\). The elliptic curve \\(A/F\\) is non-CM by Proposition 11. Its mod-\\(5\\) representation is decomposed generic and absolutely irreducible on \\(G_{F(\\zeta_5)}\\), by the pair \\((m,l)=(3,5)\\) in that proposition. Since \\(\\zeta_5\\notin F\\) by Proposition 3, Proposition 7 supplies a cuspidal weight-zero representation of \\(\\mathrm{PGL}_2(\\mathbf A_F)\\) realizing \\(V_\\eta\\).</p><p>Apply Proposition 6 successively as follows:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\n\\text{automorphic input}&amp;\\text{coefficient prime}&amp;\n\\text{system lifted}&amp;\\text{residual input}\\\\ \\hline\nV_\\eta&amp;p&amp;V_\\chi&amp;(m,l)=(3,p)\\\\\nV_\\chi&amp;3&amp;V_\\psi&amp;(m,l)=(p,3)\\\\\nV_\\psi&amp;p&amp;V_1&amp;H^1(B)\\bmod p.\n\\end{array}\n\\]</p><p>For each row, Proposition 5 identifies the residual representations. Proposition 11 supplies decomposed genericity and absolute irreducibility over the cyclotomic extension. Proposition 3 supplies the determinant, finite ramification, potential semistability, and labeled weights. Proposition 12 makes the previous automorphy available at the next coefficient prime.</p><p>At a potentially good ordinary place, both systems are potentially crystalline and potentially ordinary of weight zero, by Proposition 10. At a potentially good supersingular place, both are potentially crystalline and neither is potentially ordinary. In either case Proposition 12 gives zero monodromy in the input automorphic parameter.</p><p>At a potentially totally toric place, neither local Galois representation is potentially crystalline, by Proposition 10, and Proposition 12 gives automorphic ordinarity. Thus the required local alternative in Proposition 6 holds at every place above the coefficient prime.</p><p>Each application preserves cuspidality, weight zero, and trivial central character, giving the input for the next row. The last row proves automorphy of \\(V_1=H^1(B)\\), using Proposition 4.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Lemma 9.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L'/L\\) be a cyclic extension of prime degree between CM fields.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  r:G_L\\to\\mathrm{GL}_2(\\overline{\\mathbf Q}_l)\n  \\]\n  be continuous, with \\(r|_{G_{L'}}\\) absolutely irreducible.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(r|_{G_{L'}}\\) is realized by a cuspidal weight-zero regular algebraic representation \\(\\Pi\\) of \\(\\mathrm{GL}_2(\\mathbf A_{L'})\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The representation \\(r\\) is realized by a cuspidal weight-zero regular algebraic representation of \\(\\mathrm{GL}_2(\\mathbf A_L)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(\\sigma\\in\\operatorname{Gal}(L'/L)\\), the representations \\(\\Pi^\\sigma\\) and \\(\\Pi\\) have the same good-place Hecke polynomials because their associated Galois representation extends to \\(G_L\\). Strong multiplicity one makes \\(\\Pi\\) Galois invariant. Cyclic base change gives a cuspidal representation \\(\\pi\\) of\n\\(\\mathrm{GL}_2(\\mathbf A_L)\\) whose base change is \\(\\Pi\\) [28, 3].</p><p>Every archimedean place is complex, so its local extension is trivial. Thus \\(\\pi\\) is regular algebraic of weight zero. Good-place compatibility with base change and Chebotarev give\n\\[\n\\bigl(r_\\iota(\\pi)|_{G_{L'}}\\bigr)^{\\mathrm{ss}}\n\\simeq r|_{G_{L'}}.\n\\]\nThe right side is absolutely irreducible. The restriction on the left is therefore already irreducible and isomorphic to it without semisimplification.</p><p>Choose this isomorphism so the representations agree on \\(G_{L'}\\). For \\(g\\in G_L\\), their matrices differ by a scalar because they induce the same conjugation on the absolutely irreducible representation of \\(G_{L'}\\). Schur’s lemma makes these scalars a character\n\\[\n\\delta:\\operatorname{Gal}(L'/L)\\to\n\\overline{\\mathbf Q}_l^\\times,\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\nr_\\iota(\\pi)\\simeq r\\otimes\\delta.\n\\]\nTwist by the finite-order Hecke character corresponding to \\(\\delta^{-1}\\). This removes the discrepancy while preserving cuspidality and weight zero. Compatibility with Galois twisting follows at good places and everywhere by Chebotarev.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 5, "cite"], [1, 6, "cite"], [1, 8, "cite"], [1, 9, "cite"], [1, 19, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [2, 3, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [3, 4, "cite"], [4, 1, "cite"], [5, 9, "cite"], [5, 10, "cite"], [5, 12, "cite"], [5, 22, "cite"], [6, 7, "cite"], [6, 8, "cite"], [7, 5, "cite"], [7, 8, "cite"], [8, 5, "cite"], [9, 0, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [9, 11, "cite"], [9, 12, "cite"], [9, 19, "cite"], [9, 21, "cite"], [9, 23, "cite"], [9, 25, "cite"], [9, 26, "cite"], [9, 27, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [10, 21, "cite"], [10, 23, "cite"], [10, 27, "cite"], [11, 21, "cite"], [11, 27, "cite"], [12, 11, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [13, 19, "cite"], [13, 21, "cite"], [13, 27, "cite"], [14, 27, "cite"], [15, 0, "cite"], [16, 19, "cite"], [17, 16, "cite"], [18, 16, "cite"], [19, 0, "cite"], [19, 21, "cite"], [19, 25, "cite"], [19, 27, "cite"], [20, 19, "cite"], [21, 0, "cite"], [21, 27, "cite"], [22, 21, "cite"], [23, 21, "cite"], [24, 21, "cite"], [25, 27, "cite"], [26, 25, "cite"], [27, 0, "cite"], [28, 0, "cite"]]});
